WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.368
Thresholds, and why naive gates flake.

2
00:00:02.648 --> 00:00:06.701
The obvious gate is “fail if the pass rate is below 90 percent.

3
00:00:06.981 --> 00:00:11.291
” On a live suite, that gate is a statistical test with a hidden error rate.

4
00:00:11.571 --> 00:00:17.203
With 60 questions and a true pass rate of 92 percent, the standard error is about 3.

5
00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:26.869
5 points, so the observed rate falls below 90 percent on about one run in five (54 or more passes out of 60 are needed) with nothing wrong.

6
00:00:27.149 --> 00:00:31.181
Engineers learn to rerun until green, and then the gate catches nothing.

7
00:00:31.461 --> 00:00:42.320
The pytest docs describe flaky tests as tests that fail intermittently without a clear reason, and name the cost: people stop trusting results and real failures get overlooked.

8
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.459
Their suggestions translate directly to evals.

9
00:00:45.739 --> 00:00:49.088
Gate CI on the reliable suite and monitor the others.

10
00:00:49.368 --> 00:00:59.651
Use a temporary quarantine (an xfail mark that doesn’t fail the build) for a known-flaky case, but don’t leave it there, since the docs warn against keeping that permanently.

11
00:00:59.931 --> 00:01:11.472
Plugins that rerun failures exist; for evals, automatic reruns are an especially bad idea, because rerunning a noisy measurement until it passes is selecting on the outcome.
